WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.400
People have got so many
misconceptions about the Middle Ages,

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:06.960
starting with the idea
of being called Dark Ages,

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.960
where we don't even know
where that comes from.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:12.000
And also this idea that,
in terms of astronomy,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.920
the medieval people thought that
the Earth was flat - they never did.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.120
We have no evidence of that.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:29.920
Comets are these strange objects
which are very unpredictable

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.520
in the way they behave,
they can explode, they can split -

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.720
all sorts of strange things
can happen.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.720
So we need observations,
we need a record of that -

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.760
of what was seen, when.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.560
And usually I only work with
observations that are done

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.520
in the last maybe 50 years,
and this project uses observations

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.720
of people that lived
a thousand years ago,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.160
it's almost like I can pick up
the phone and call one of these monks

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.160
a thousand years ago and ask them,
"Hey, my computer tells me

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.120
there should have been a comet in the
sky on this date, did you see it?"

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.640
The Anglo-Saxons really tried
to understand the cosmos.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.640
If the Anglo-Saxons saw a comet
as an omen or a portent -

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:18.680
which also was the case,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:22.200
for telling and predicting
the death of an important king

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.160
or war, famine,
this doesn't mean that we're not

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.600
interested in understanding
how that comet worked.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.960
Venerable Bede was one of the
most influential and important

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.480
intellectual scholars
from the Middle Ages.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.920
He was a monk from Northumbria

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.680
and he lived between the end of the
7th, beginning of the 8th Centuries.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.760
And I guess some of the myths that we
have about the Middle Ages as well

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.040
comes from this difficulty of
accessing the language, which can be

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.600
either Latin or Old English or any
other medieval vernacular language

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.240
and accessing his material
isn't easy.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:19.280
So we try to work with comets
that have periods of orbits

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.120
around the sun, that means that
they came back maybe ten times

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.720
since the middle ages.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.960
But there are comets the come
from much larger distances,

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.040
from a region called the
Oort Cloud and those

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.640
come back once every million years,
so if one of those comets was seen

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.840
by the monks, I will not be able
to reproduce that observation.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.280
But it will be important to know
that things were seen in the sky

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.400
because it gives us an idea of how
many of these comets were

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.840
visiting the Earth
as a function of time.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.520
Medieval monks described comets in
quite a bit of detail and sometimes

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.400
they give the month, the date,
sometimes even the hour on which

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.720
the comet was seen and occurred.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.040
So we get lots of information
that is useful.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.880
What I was very surprised
about is how much

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.160
there is still to look for
in these texts.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.800
I thought, "Well, this stuff has been
written a thousand years ago,

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.800
we probably know everything
there is to know about it"

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:23.720
but it's not true.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:28.040
Since I started working on this
project, I've been learning that

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:31.960
we're still discovering manuscripts,
they're still being translated -

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.600
so there is still material
that is not known.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.840
So there's a lot of things
to discover.

